好的，我们开始。Let’s go through the steps one by one. First, we define the variables. Let A represent the set of all students in the class, which is called the universal set (全集). Let B represent the subset (子集) of students who play basketball, and let C represent the subset (子集) of students who play soccer. We are told that every student plays at least one sport, so the union (并集) of B and C equals A. Mathematically, this means A = B ∪ C. Next, we are given that the number of students who play both sports, which is the intersection (交集) B ∩ C, is 5. We are also told that the number of students who play basketball only, which is B
minus C (差集), is 12, and the number who play soccer only, C minus B, is 8. Now, we need to find the total number of students in the class. To do this, we use the principle of inclusion-exclusion (容斥原理). The formula is |A| = |B| + |C| - |B ∩ C|. But we do not directly have |B| and |C|. Instead, we break them down: |B| = |B only| + |B ∩ C| = 12 + 5 = 17. Similarly, |C| = |C only| + |B ∩ C| = 8 + 5 = 13. Then plugging into the formula: |A| = 17 + 13 - 5 = 25. So, the total number of students is 25. Let us verify: Students who play only basketball (12) plus only soccer (8) plus both (5) gives 12 + 8 + 5 = 25. Perfect! Yay! 好极了！

